<TopicPage slug="subjects/criminology/foundational/changing-awareness-of-crime">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the opening lesson of Criminology, and it starts from an uncomfortable
    observation: <strong>what the public "knows" about crime is mostly manufactured</strong>
    — by what gets reported, what gets recorded, and what the media chooses to
    dramatise. You need nothing from earlier lessons, only a willingness to distrust a
    headline. The lesson walks the first four assessment criteria of WJEC Unit 1: the
    types of crime (AC1.1), why some go unreported and what that costs (AC1.2–1.3), how
    media represents crime (AC1.4), and how we even measure the thing (AC1.6).
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>separate <em>deviance</em> from <em>crime</em>, and explain why "crime" is a changing legal category rather than a fixed list;</li>
    <li>analyse the unit's crime types — white-collar, moral, state, technological, individual — by victims, offenders and level of public awareness;</li>
    <li>explain the personal and social reasons crimes go unreported, and trace the consequences in both directions;</li>
    <li>describe how media representation distorts crime, including the moral-panic mechanism;</li>
    <li>evaluate the two instruments that count crime — police records and victimisation surveys — and say exactly what each one cannot see.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The fraud paradox, and what counts as a crime</h2>
  <p>
    A single example shows why the unit opens here. In England and Wales, fraud —
    deceiving people and organisations out of money or property — has for years been
    among the <em>most commonly experienced</em> offence types in the national
    victimisation survey, running to millions of incidents a year; yet ask anyone to
    picture "crime" and they picture burglary or violence. The mismatch is not
    stupidity. It is the product of reporting rates (most fraud is never brought to the
    police), of recording systems (online fraud crossing force boundaries fits police
    counting awkwardly), and of media selection (a mugging makes a story; a phishing
    email makes a paragraph). "Changing awareness of crime" means learning to see the
    machinery that produces the picture — because policy, policing priorities and
    personal precaution are all set by the picture, not the reality.
  </p>
  <p>
    The machinery starts with a distinction sociology insists on. <strong>Deviance</strong>
    is a violation of social norms — walking backwards to class, breaking a dress code;
    it is not always punished and not necessarily bad. <strong>Crime</strong> is a
    behaviour that violates official law and is punishable through formal sanctions —
    law being the rules a political authority adopts and enforces. The two overlap but
    are not the same set, and the gap between them moves: the sociologist's standard
    example is the civil-rights movement, whose activists deliberately broke laws —
    refusing to give up a bus seat, sitting at segregated counters — acts that were
    crimes when committed and are now recognised as protests against unjust law.
    Everything on this page lives somewhere on that deviance–crime–awareness map.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "crime" is a fixed list handed down by nature. What
  counts as a crime is a legal category that changes with time and place — same-sex acts
  were crimes in Britain until 1967; witchcraft was once a capital offence; corporate
  practices legal in one jurisdiction are crimes in another; and Rosa Parks's refusal was
  a crime at the moment it mattered. The unit's job is precisely to keep the
  <em>legal</em>, <em>social</em> and <em>publicly imagined</em> boundaries of crime
  separate in your mind.</p>

  <h2>Types of crime you must analyse</h2>
  <p>
    AC1.1 names a set of crime types, and the unit requires you to analyse each along
    the same four axes: what criminal offence (if any) is committed, who the victims
    are, who the offenders are, and — the axis that ties Unit 1 together — the
    <strong>level of public awareness</strong>. The everyday sorting most people carry
    (violent crime against persons, nonviolent crime against property) is the starting
    point; the unit's types are cut across it, by <em>who</em> offends and <em>how
    visible</em> the offence is.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>White-collar crime</strong> — committed by the respectable in the course of work: organised, corporate and professional variants. Fraud, tax evasion, price-fixing, insider trading and corporate negligence causing harm. Edwin Sutherland coined the term in 1939 precisely to show that crime is not concentrated among the poor. It routinely loses the coverage contest with street crime — yet a single pension fund fraud can devastate thousands of families' savings at once. Its victims are diffuse — thousands of small losses with no single outraged victim — which is exactly why awareness stays low.</li>
    <li><strong>Moral crimes</strong> — acts against perceived standards of decency: prostitution, vagrancy, illegal gambling, assisted suicide, under-age drinking. "Victimless" is contested — defenders say no identifiable person is harmed; critics point to the exploitation and social damage that shadow the trade — and that contest drives whether anyone calls the police. Whether such acts should be crimes at all is a live debate the unit expects you to argue, not assume.</li>
    <li><strong>State crimes</strong> — offences by or with the acquiescence of governments: genocide, torture, imprisonment without trial, human-rights violations, state corporate crime. The offender writes the rules, so awareness depends on outsiders — journalists, whistleblowers, international bodies — which makes the awareness question existential rather than academic.</li>
    <li><strong>Technological (e-)crime</strong> — hacking, phishing, identity theft, online fraud, malware. Borderless, fast-moving, and frequently never detected by the victim until long after; offences may fall under the Computer Misuse Act 1990 or ordinary fraud statutes, and many incidents are never reported because reporting seems hopeless.</li>
    <li><strong>Individual crimes</strong> — hate crime, honour crime, domestic abuse: offences against a person precisely because of who they are or what relationship they are in. Hate crime shows the awareness machinery at its starkest: survey estimates of victimisation run to hundreds of thousands of people a year, while official recorded counts sit in the low thousands — the gap made of fear of retribution, dread of a grinding legal process, and the fact that a court must find clear proof the offence was <em>motivated</em> by hostility, not merely accompanied by it. The term itself only became an official legal category in the 1980s, and public awareness has been reshaped by named cases — the murders of Brandon Teena in 1993 and Matthew Shepard in 1998 reshaped awareness around gender expression and sexual orientation. Awareness, here, is recent and constructed.</li>
    <li><strong>Domestic abuse</strong> deserves its own attention as the unit's recurring case study: criminal across several statutes, historically treated as private, transformed over fifty years by feminist campaigning, dedicated legislation (from the Domestic Violence and Matrimonial Proceedings Act 1976 to the Domestic Abuse Act 2021) and compulsory recording — a before-and-after demonstration that <em>awareness is manufactured and can be re-manufactured</em>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the spread of public awareness across these types is
    enormous. Domestic abuse, after decades of campaigns and legal reform, is now
    loudly visible even though reporting remains partial; corporate fraud may cost the
    economy billions yet barely registers as "crime" in the public mind, because its
    victims are scattered shareholders and its offender a respectable logo. Awareness is
    not a by-product of harm; it is manufactured by reporting, recording and media —
    which is exactly why the next two sections matter.
  </p>

  <h2>Why crimes go unreported — and what that does</h2>
  <p>
    The unit divides the reasons in two. <strong>Personal reasons</strong>: fear (of the
    offender, or of not being believed, or of retaliation), shame, disinterest, or
    feeling not really affected — "it was only a phone". <strong>Social and cultural
    reasons</strong>: lack of knowledge that a crime occurred or was a crime (a scam
    that looks like bad luck; coercion that looks like a relationship), complexity
    ("who do I even tell about a frozen crypto account?"), lack of media interest, lack
    of current public concern — and culture-bound crimes such as honour killing or
    accusations of witchcraft, where reporting would betray the community itself. Each
    reason predicts a different remedy: fear responds to victim support and anonymity;
    ignorance responds to campaigns; complexity responds to specialised reporting
    channels — which is how Action Fraud and the NPCC's True Vision (for hate crime)
    came to exist.
  </p>
  <p>
    The consequences cut both ways. Unreported crime breeds a <em>ripple effect</em>
    (one offender's harm spreads through victims, families, neighbourhoods), lets
    behaviour <em>decriminalise</em> in practice, skews <em>police prioritisation</em>,
    and swells the pool of <em>unrecorded crime</em>. But the unit also asks for the
    positives: silence can precede <em>cultural change</em> (what was shamed becomes
    reportable — as with domestic abuse), and under-reporting can drive <em>legal and
    procedural change</em> once the dark figure is finally exposed. Worked through the
    domestic abuse case: the dark figure was exposed by surveys and campaigns in the
    1970s–90s; awareness rose; reporting rose — and the recorded numbers <em>went
    up</em>, which is the crucial subtlety. A rising crime statistic can mean rising
    crime <em>or rising confidence to report</em>, and confusing the two is the standard
    error this unit exists to prevent.
  </p>

  <h2>The media's version of crime</h2>
  <p>
    Newspapers, television, film, electronic gaming, social media and music all portray
    crime — fictionally and factually — and the portrayal is systematically
    unrepresentative: violent and sexual offences are over-shown, the common crimes
    under-shown. Content analyses going back to the 1970s (Chibnall's work on British
    crime reporting) found recurring news values — immediacy, dramatisation,
    personalisation, and the structural violence of "a good story" — that distort the
    picture independent of any editor's intention. Stanley Cohen's <em>Folk Devils and
    Moral Panics</em> (1972) supplies the classic mechanism: exaggerated reporting
    creates a <strong>folk devil</strong> — a group cast as the symbol of a society's
    fears (the 1960s Mods and Rockers were his case) — public concern spikes, and
    responses and punishments escalate beyond what the trend justifies; once the label
    exists, more deviance is noticed, reported and created, completing the loop. The
    measurable result is the <strong>perception gap</strong>: research on media
    audiences finds consistently that people who closely follow crime news estimate
    crime rates as higher than the statistics show and feel more fearful — and in the
    United States, where violent crime has declined from its early-1990s peak,
    majorities nonetheless keep telling pollsters crime is getting worse. England and
    Wales shows the same shape: the victimisation survey tracked a long fall in
    conventional crime from the mid-1990s, while public belief in rising crime barely
    moved. Social media now shortens the panic cycle to days and lets unverified clips
    stand in for statistics.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the amount of crime in the news mirrors the amount of
  crime on the street — so if the media are full of stabbings, stabbings must be rising.
  News values select for the dramatic, the visual and the personal: violent and sexual
  offences are chronically over-shown and the most common offences under-shown, in
  analyses stretching back to the 1970s. Coverage measures what makes a story, not what
  makes a statistic — which is precisely why the moral-panic mechanism can run without
  any underlying trend at all.</p>

  <h2>Measuring the unmeasurable</h2>
  <Widget no="1" caption="the measurement gap, step by step — why no single number can be 'the' crime rate" widget="equationStep" config={gapCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the widget walks the arithmetic of the
    <strong>dark figure</strong>. Of 100 real incidents, roughly 40 reach the police as
    reports — the Crime Survey for England and Wales has found for decades that only
    around four in ten of the crimes its respondents experience are ever brought to
    police attention. Of those, recording rules and evidence tests shave the official
    count down further (perhaps 35). The 60 that never got past the victim are
    invisible to every table built from police data. This is why the field uses two
    instruments with deliberately different biases. <strong>Police-recorded
    crime</strong> is an administrative count: complete for murder (there are no
    unreported murders for long), sensitive to recording practice and priorities, and
    needed for local operational data. The <strong>Crime Survey for England and
    Wales</strong> asks tens of thousands of households directly about victimisation,
    reported or not: it is immune to recording-rule changes and captures the dark
    figure — but it misses "victimless" crimes, businesses and children under 16 (in
    the main sample), and it inherits survey error like any questionnaire.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> police-recorded crime is the real number and the Crime
  Survey is an opinion poll about it. The relationship runs the other way round: the
  administrative count inherits every filter in the system — whether the victim told
  anyone, whether the force recorded it — while the survey measures experienced
  victimisation directly and is immune to recording-rule changes. Each instrument has
  its own blind spots (businesses and children fall outside the household survey; murder
  is essentially complete in police data), which is why the honest answer is
  triangulation, not a favourite.</p>
  <p>
    This design is not a British quirk — the same two-instrument logic runs worldwide,
    which is how you know it is the correct design. The United States pairs the FBI's
    <strong>Uniform Crime Reports</strong> (an administrative count assembled from
    around 17,000 agencies, migrating to a more detailed incident-based system, NIBRS)
    with the <strong>National Crime Victimization Survey</strong>, a self-report study
    of roughly 160,000 people a year that reliably reports a higher crime rate than the
    police count — the dark figure again — and, like every survey, misses some
    populations and depends on victim recall.
  </p>
  <p>
    Because both instruments are flawed in different directions, criminologists
    triangulate: neither is "the true rate", each has its own reliability, validity and
    ethics (AC1.6's evaluation criteria), and the honest rule is read them together, or
    not at all. The two-hundred-year history of trying is itself a lesson in
    measurement: each milestone below created a better lens and a new kind of blind
    spot.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="two centuries of trying to count crime in England and Wales" widget="timeline" config={measureTimeline} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> each milestone added a lens and a blind spot.
    Recording began with the police (1829, 1857), so it inherited every reporting
    filter from day one; the survey (1982) finally measured the dark figure directly;
    the 2002 recording standard then showed how the administrative instrument itself
    can move. Notice also the direction of travel: from counting what the system
    <em>processes</em> toward measuring what people <em>experience</em> — the same
    journey your own evaluation of AC1.6 has to make.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Distinguish deviance from crime, with one example of each and one act that is both.</li>
    <li>Why does white-collar crime stay so low in public awareness despite its scale?</li>
    <li>Recorded domestic abuse rose sharply between 2010 and 2020 in England and Wales. Give two different readings of that fact, and say what additional data you would need to choose between them.</li>
    <li>A friend says: "The police recorded 40,000 more violent crimes this year — violence is exploding." What are the first two questions you ask?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Deviance violates social norms (walking backwards to
    class); crime violates official law and carries formal sanctions (driving over the
    alcohol limit). An act that is both: the civil-rights sit-ins — norm-defying and
    law-breaking at once, and retrospectively honoured, which is the point about legal
    categories moving. (2) Its victims are diffuse — thousands of small losses with no
    single outraged victim — its offender is a respectable logo rather than a
    stereotypical criminal, and it loses the news-value contest to street crime; so the
    reporting, recording and media layers each filter it out. (3) Reading one: more
    abuse is occurring. Reading two: confidence to report rose — exactly the domestic
    abuse pattern after decades of campaigns, dedicated legislation and compulsory
    recording. To choose between them you need an instrument immune to reporting
    changes: the victimisation survey's trend, plus recording-practice evidence. (4)
    First: which instrument — and did anything about recording change (the 2002
    standard and post-2014 pushes to record what victims say both moved the count
    without any change on the street)? Second: what does the victimisation survey say
    over the same period? If the survey is flat while the administrative count jumps,
    the explosion is in the paperwork, not the streets.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Deviance violates norms; crime violates law — and the legal category moves with time and place, so "crime" is never a fixed list.</li>
    <li>The unit's crime types (white-collar, moral, state, technological, individual, domestic abuse) are analysed by offence, victims, offenders and — above all — level of public awareness.</li>
    <li>Unreported crime has personal causes (fear, shame, disinterest) and social ones (ignorance, complexity, lack of media interest, culture); its consequences include ripple effects, practical decriminalisation, skewed priorities — but also, once exposed, cultural and legal change.</li>
    <li>A rising recorded-crime statistic can mean rising crime or rising confidence to report; domestic abuse is the case study that proves awareness is manufactured and re-manufacturable.</li>
    <li>Media representation systematically over-shows violence and under-shows common crime; the moral-panic mechanism can run with no underlying trend, and heavy media consumption reliably inflates perceived crime.</li>
    <li>Two instruments, two different gaps: police-recorded crime (administrative, sensitive to recording rules) and the victimisation survey (experienced crime, immune to recording rules, blind to businesses and young children). Read them together, or not at all.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Unit 1's second half builds directly on this lens: how crime is <em>reported</em>
    in campaigns and how change is engineered (why some campaigns work and some
    backfire), and case studies of awareness raising — the domestic-abuse story
    continues there in detail. Later units then cross the bridge this one built: once
    you know how crime is counted and pictured, you can study why people offend and how
    the system responds — without ever again mistaking a recording artefact for a real
    trend.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three refinements separate a strong analysis from a competent one. First,
    <strong>recording reform as a false trend</strong>: the 2002 National Crime
    Recording Standard moved forces from counting what they judged provable toward
    counting what victims said happened; recorded violence against the person rose
    sharply in the following years with no matching rise in the survey — the cleanest
    demonstration in British statistics that the recording instrument changed while the
    underlying reality did not. In 2014 an inspection (HMIC) judged police recording
    unacceptably poor, which is another direction of the same lesson: administrative
    data measures the <em>administration</em>. Second, the <strong>gap is not
    uniform</strong>: reporting rates for vandalism are far higher than for fraud or
    sexual offences, so the dark figure is a shadow with a shape — and the shape tells
    you where interventions should aim. Third, the <strong>open frontier</strong>: much
    e-crime now bypasses both instruments — fraud reported to banks is counted in
    neither police-recorded statistics nor, until recently, the survey's main counts,
    so the fastest-growing crime category in Western societies is also the least
    measured. The unit's method — always ask which instrument, what its blind spot is,
    and who benefits from the number that gets quoted — transfers to every statistic
    you will ever be shown.
  </p>
  <p>
    A closing worked exercise rehearses that method on a claim you might read tomorrow:
    <em>"shoplifting has doubled since 2015"</em>. The disciplined questions, in order:
    which instrument says so — police-recorded theft or the survey (whose coverage of
    shop theft is weak, since businesses are outside the household sample)? Did
    recording practice change (post-2014 forces were pushed to record more, and several
    retailers began systematically reporting rather than absorbing losses)? What does
    the denominator do — retail moved toward self-scan checkouts with different loss
    profiles? And who is quoting it — a retailers' association, a police force seeking
    resources, a newspaper in a slow news week? None of this means the trend is false;
    organised shop theft genuinely rose in many jurisdictions. It means the claim's
    confidence should be calibrated to its instrument — and that calibrating,
    reflexively, is precisely the skill AC1.1–AC1.6 were designed to build: awareness
    of crime that includes awareness of how crime comes to be known at all.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> WJEC Level 3 Applied Diploma in Criminology, Unit 1 (AC1.1–AC1.6), 2022 specification; ONS, Crime Survey for England and Wales methodology; Home Office, National Crime Recording Standard (2002); HMIC, Crime-recording inspection (2014); Cohen, Folk Devils and Moral Panics (1972); Chibnall, Law-and-Order News (1977); Sutherland, "White-Collar Criminality" (1939). Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>

